Compact EEG decoders summarise each window as the log band power of learned
spatial filters, which carry the covariance between channels only implicitly.
We add a tangent-space branch beside that stem, mapping each window's shrunk
covariance to the tangent space at a reference the network maintains from its
input alone. It needs no labels and keeps adapting at test time. Across
five cohorts and 51 participants, three seeds each, the branch raises balanced
accuracy on four. It adds $0.064$ on a 20-participant
motor-execution cohort held out from development ($p=8.8\times10^{-5}$, higher
in 20 of 20), $0.040$ on BCI Competition IV-2a ($p=0.020$), $0.021$ on a second
exoskeleton cohort ($p=0.047$) and $0.045$ on a lower-limb exoskeleton cohort
($p=0.22$); 40 of the 43 participants improve. On those four it
has the highest mean accuracy among eight published decoders trained untuned in
the same pipeline. A classical tangent-space classifier with no network falls
$0.116$ below it on the longitudinal exoskeleton cohort and matches it on the
held-out cohort. On the fifth cohort the branch costs $0.175$, and that failure
is predictable before training: the reference is a running mean, safe only while
its effective memory $\mu=N/m$ outlasts a class block. Blocks last 214 windows
there against a memory of 160, and 1 to 18 where the branch helps. Moving the
memory across the block length switches the effect off and on: $+0.045$ at 160
windows, $+0.048$ at 40 and $-0.076$ at 8. A pre-specified prescription for the rate itself was
falsified.
